---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {cacheSidebar} from '../../../data/cache-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Eviction Policies" sidebar={cacheSidebar}
            description="Four eviction behaviours: TTL by access, TTL by write, max entries, and max memory">

    <h1>Eviction Policies</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Four eviction behaviours — use one, several, or all together.
    </p>

    <h2>Expire after access</h2>

    <p>Evicts entries that haven't been read or written within a time-to-live window. The timer resets on every
        access:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.cache.fastCache
import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.minutes
import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.seconds

val cache = fastCache<String, String> {
    expireAfterAccess(5.minutes)
}

cache.put("session", "abc123")

// Reading resets the TTL
cache.get("session")  // resets the 5-minute timer

// After 5 minutes of no access, the entry is evicted`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <Aside>Eviction is processed in the background loop, not on the calling thread. The TTL counts from the loop pass
        that sees the access, so an entry can be served up to its TTL plus two loop periods (default: 50ms each).
        TTLs run on a monotonic clock: a change of the system time neither expires an entry early nor keeps it longer.
        Time the machine spends suspended may not count; <code>timeSource(...)</code> in the builder swaps the clock
        for tests.
    </Aside>

    <p>Odd values never throw: a zero or negative TTL expires the entry at the next pass, a positive one below 1 ms
        counts as 1 ms, and <code>Duration.INFINITE</code> never expires. A loop delay below 1 ms runs as 1 ms.</p>

    <h2>Expire after write</h2>

    <p>Evicts entries that haven't been <em>written</em> within a TTL window. Unlike <code>expireAfterAccess</code>,
        reading an entry does NOT reset the timer — only a write does (<code>put()</code>, <code>getOrPut()</code>
        on a miss, or a stored refresh result):</p>

    <Code code={`val cache = fastCache<String, String> {
    expireAfterWrite(5.minutes)
}

cache.put("token", "abc123")

// Reading does NOT reset the TTL
cache.get("token")  // timer keeps ticking from the original put

// Only a new put resets the TTL
cache.put("token", "xyz789")  // timer resets now`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>A key read in every pass still expires on time: only a pending write spares an entry from write-based
        expiry.</p>

    <p>Use <code>expireAfterWrite</code> when the data has a known staleness window regardless of how often
        it's read — API tokens, DNS lookups, config values fetched from a remote service, exchange rates.</p>

    <Aside>If you need stale data to be refreshed rather than evicted, see
        <a href="/ultra/cache/refresh">Background Refresh</a> — it serves the old value while reloading in the
        background.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Max entries</h2>

    <p>Caps the number of entries. When the limit is exceeded, the least-recently-accessed entries are evicted first
        (LRU):</p>

    <Code code={`val cache = fastCache<String, String> {
    maxEntries(100)
}

// Over 100 entries, the next loop pass evicts
// the least recently accessed ones`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><code>maxEntries</code> and <code>maxMemoryUsage</code> are soft bounds: an entry read or written in every loop
        pass is not evicted by them, so under such load the cache holds up to the number of distinct keys touched
        within one pass.</p>

    <h2>Max memory usage</h2>

    <p>Caps the sum of the estimated sizes of all entries. When the budget is exceeded, the least recently accessed
        entries are evicted first (LRU):</p>

    <Code code={`val cache = fastCache<String, MyData> {
    maxMemoryUsage(50L * 1024 * 1024)  // 50 MB budget, default estimator
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>An entry is sized when it is written: the estimator runs once for the key and once for the value, on the
        maintenance loop. Reads reuse that size, so a value changed in place keeps the size it had at its put.</p>

    <p><strong>An entry larger than the whole budget</strong> is evicted alone at the next pass and never counted; the
        other entries stay. A read does not spare it, only a newer pending write, which is sized anew. The cost: such a
        value is never cached, also a small one whose estimate is inflated (see sampling below).</p>

    <h3>The default estimator</h3>

    <p><code>ObjectSizeEstimator()</code> walks the value and counts primitives by size; a string as header,
        reference, array header and 2 bytes per char; an array as header plus its elements; a collection as header
        plus one reference per element (a map two), plus what the elements hold; any other object as header plus one
        reference per field, plus what the fields hold. Fields come from reflection on the JVM and from own properties
        on JS. Native has no reflection: any other object is charged a flat 32 bytes, whatever it holds. An object
        reached twice within one estimate is counted once.</p>

    <p>Its cost is bounded by its configuration, not by the value:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Breadth first, with a cap.</strong> At most 10 000 objects are visited (<code>maxObjects</code>).
            Strings and primitive arrays are sized from their length and do not count. Past the cap, any other object
            is charged a flat header and two references (32 bytes) without looking inside.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Sampling.</strong> A collection, map, array or object with more than 64 elements or fields
            (<code>sampleSize</code>) is sampled at up to 64 fixed positions, and the rest is charged by their average.
            A collection without index access (a set, a linked list, a map) is sampled over its first 1 024 elements
            only (<code>sampleWindow</code>). The positions follow the golden ratio by default, never random, so the
            same value always gets the same estimate.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Never throws.</strong> The walk is iterative. A part whose walk fails is charged flat and counted
            in the loop's failure line (<code>FastCache size estimate (part charged flat) failed N time(s)</code>).
            Sizes saturate at <code>Long.MAX_VALUE</code> and are never negative.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>One estimate is at most 10 000 visits, each handing on at most 64 elements (a map: 64 keys and 64 values), with
        at most 1 024 iterator steps per collection. Measured on the JVM: 10 000 strings 0.03 ms, a million strings in
        nested lists 15 to 35 ms. Not covered by that bound: listing a JS object's own properties, a <code>size</code>
        that is linear (a concurrent queue), iterating a hash map or set over its empty slots (a once huge
        <code>HashMap</code> costs its capacity), and user code in a <code>size</code>, <code>get</code>,
        <code>iterator</code> or getter (a lazy ORM collection may load, or throw). Estimating runs outside the cache's
        lock, once per written key and pass.</p>

    <h3>It is an estimate</h3>

    <p>Exact sizes would mean walking everything, on every write. The default trades accuracy for that bound:</p>

    <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Where</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Errs</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-white font-semibold">Why</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Sampling</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">either way</td>
                <td class="py-2">Each sample counts about <code>n / 64</code> times: a large element is over-counted
                    when sampled, missed when not. Nested sampled levels multiply (three levels of 1 000: up to about
                    3 800 times). A set, map or linked list is not seen past its first 1 024 elements.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Shared objects</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">high</td>
                <td class="py-2">Reached through sampled elements, one counts with their weight (6 400 holders of one
                    string: 7.6 times). Key and value, and different entries, are sized separately: what they share
                    counts in each.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">The cap</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">low</td>
                <td class="py-2">An object past it is charged 32 bytes, whatever it holds (100 orders of 100 lines of
                    15 objects: 0.63). A cut empty collection or one-field object is up to 16 bytes high.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Reachability</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">high</td>
                <td class="py-2">Everything reachable counts, up to the cap: a reference to a service or a coroutine
                    <code>Job</code> is charged for all behind it.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">JVM</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">low</td>
                <td class="py-2">A map entry is about 48 bytes, 16 are charged; a boxed element its raw size; a type
                    JPMS keeps closed (<code>Instant</code>, <code>UUID</code>, <code>BigDecimal</code>) an empty
                    object.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">JVM</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">high</td>
                <td class="py-2">The defaults assume 8-byte references and 16-byte headers (HotSpot with compressed
                    references: 4 and 12) and 2 bytes per char (a Latin-1 string stores 1).
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">JS</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">mixed</td>
                <td class="py-2">Every number 8 bytes. Equal strings count once (no string identity). A JS
                    <code>Map</code> or <code>Set</code> is charged an empty object; typed arrays and
                    <code>ArrayBuffer</code> their byte length. Reading properties runs getters and
                    <code>Proxy</code> traps.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Native</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">either way</td>
                <td class="py-2">Any object other than a string, array, collection or map: a flat 32 bytes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Writes only</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">either way</td>
                <td class="py-2">A value changed in place keeps its old size until it is put again.</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <Aside>„It is called FastCache, not ExactCache." With the default estimator, <code>maxMemoryUsage</code> is
        <strong>not a memory bound for data an attacker shapes</strong>: the sample positions are fixed, so a value can
        be built to be estimated far too high (it is never cached) or far too low (large elements where no sample
        looks, or past a set's first 1 024). Use it as a pressure signal, or bring your own estimator.
    </Aside>

    <h3>Build your own estimator</h3>

    <p><code>ObjectSizeEstimator</code> is a <code>fun interface</code>, so a lambda will do. For data from outside,
        size it from what the application already knows (a payload's length, the byte count of the serialized form)
        and cap the count with <code>maxEntries</code> as well:</p>

    <Code code={`class Rendered(val html: String, val byteCount: Int)

val cache = fastCache<String, Rendered> {
    maxEntries(10_000)
    maxMemoryUsage(64L * 1024 * 1024) { obj ->
        when (obj) {
            is Rendered -> 64L + obj.byteCount       // the value: a size we already know
            is String -> 40L + 2L * obj.length       // the key
            else -> 0L                               // null
        }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The contract of <code>fun estimate(obj: Any?): Long</code>:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>It is called separately for the key and for the value, each as <code>Any?</code>, so it must handle
            <code>null</code>. The entry's size is the sum.
        </li>
        <li>Only on writes (a read of an entry not sized yet is the exception), on the cache's maintenance loop,
            outside the cache's lock. Keep it cheap: a slow estimate holds up the whole maintenance pass.
        </li>
        <li>Thread-safe, if one instance is shared by several caches: their loops can run at the same time.</li>
        <li>It may throw: the failure is counted in the loop's failure line, a new entry is charged
            <code>fallbackSize</code> (default 1 024 bytes), an existing one keeps its size. A negative result counts
            as 0.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>Other ways in: <code>maxMemoryUsage(bytes, estimator, fallbackSize)</code> sets the fallback (zero or below
        counts as the default), and <code>addBehaviour(FastCache.MaxMemoryUsageBehaviour(bytes, estimator,
        fallbackSize))</code> adds the behaviour directly. There is no weigher that sees key and value together, and
        the default (<code>ObjectSizeEstimatorImpl</code>) is final; a lambda that delegates to it loses the per-part
        failure count.</p>

    <p>Or tune the default. Larger samples and a larger cap buy accuracy with time:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.cache.ObjectSizeEstimator
import io.peekandpoke.ultra.cache.ObjectSizeEstimatorImpl.EstimatorConfig
import io.peekandpoke.ultra.cache.ObjectSizeEstimatorImpl.SamplePick

val estimator = ObjectSizeEstimator(
    EstimatorConfig(
        objectHeader = 12L,     // default 16
        arrayHeader = 16L,      // default 24
        pointerSize = 4L,       // default 8 (HotSpot, compressed references)
        sampleSize = 64,        // elements sampled per collection
        sampleWindow = 1_024,   // window for collections without index access
        maxObjects = 10_000,    // objects visited per estimate
        samplePick = SamplePick.GoldenRatio,  // or EvenlySpaced, a fixed stride
    )
)

val cache = fastCache<String, MyData> {
    maxMemoryUsage(50L * 1024 * 1024, estimator)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Odd settings fall back: a <code>sampleSize</code> or <code>maxObjects</code> below 1 counts as 1, a
        <code>sampleWindow</code> below the sample size as the sample size.</p>

    <h2>Combining behaviors</h2>

    <p>Stack multiple behaviors for fine-grained control. All active behaviors are checked during each eviction
        cycle:</p>

    <Code code={`val cache = fastCache<String, ExpensiveResult> {
    expireAfterAccess(10.minutes)     // remove stale entries
    maxEntries(5000)                   // cap on count (soft)
    maxMemoryUsage(                    // budget in estimated bytes
        maxMemorySize = 100L * 1024 * 1024,
        estimator = ObjectSizeEstimator(),
    )
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Custom loop delay</h2>

    <p>The background eviction loop runs every 50ms by default. Adjust it for your use case:</p>

    <Code code={`import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.milliseconds
import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.seconds

// Faster eviction checks (more CPU)
val responsive = fastCache<String, String>(loopDelay = 10.milliseconds) {
    expireAfterAccess(1.seconds)
}

// Slower checks (less CPU)
val relaxed = fastCache<String, String>(loopDelay = 1.seconds) {
    expireAfterAccess(5.minutes)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Custom coroutine scope</h2>

    <p>By default the eviction loop runs in <code>Cache.defaultCoroutineScope</code>, one scope on
        <code>Dispatchers.Default</code> shared by all caches. Pass a custom scope if you need lifecycle control:</p>

    <Code code={`import kotlinx.coroutines.CoroutineScope
import kotlinx.coroutines.Dispatchers

val scope = CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.Default)

val cache = fastCache<String, String>(scope = scope) {
    expireAfterAccess(5.minutes)
}

// When the scope is cancelled, the eviction loop stops`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The loop holds its cache weakly: once nothing else references the cache, the loop ends.</p>

    <h2>All behaviours at a glance</h2>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 text-white font-semibold">Behaviour</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-white font-semibold">Trigger</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-white font-semibold">Strategy</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2"><code>expireAfterAccess</code></td>
                <td class="py-2">No read or write within TTL</td>
                <td class="py-2">Time-based, resets on read + write</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2"><code>expireAfterWrite</code></td>
                <td class="py-2">No write within TTL</td>
                <td class="py-2">Time-based, resets on write only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2"><code>maxEntries</code></td>
                <td class="py-2">Entry count exceeds limit</td>
                <td class="py-2">LRU (least recently accessed)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2"><code>maxMemoryUsage</code></td>
                <td class="py-2">Estimated memory exceeds budget</td>
                <td class="py-2">LRU (least recently accessed)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2"><a href="/ultra/cache/refresh" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"><code>refreshAfterWrite</code></a>
                </td>
                <td class="py-2">Write age exceeds soft TTL</td>
                <td class="py-2">Async reload; optional hard TTL evicts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2"><a href="/ultra/cache/observability" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"><code>onEviction</code></a>
                </td>
                <td class="py-2">Another behaviour evicts an entry</td>
                <td class="py-2">Observer — fires callback</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="py-2"><a href="/ultra/cache/observability" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"><code>statistics</code></a>
                </td>
                <td class="py-2">Every lookup, put and eviction</td>
                <td class="py-2">Observer — counts hits, misses, puts, evictions</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4">
        <a href="/ultra/cache/refresh"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            Background Refresh &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
